# Task 9 – Introduce Abstraction

## Objective
To use abstract classes and interfaces in Java.

## Abstract Class
Vehicle is an abstract class because it represents a general
vehicle and defines the common structure.

The calculateFee() method is abstract and is implemented
by its subclasses.

## Interface
Chargeable is an interface that defines the chargingFee()
method for electric vehicles.

## Difference
An abstract class can contain instance variables,
constructors, concrete methods and abstract methods.

An interface defines a contract that implementing classes
must follow. A class can implement multiple interfaces.

## Multiple Behaviours
ElectricCar extends Vehicle and implements Chargeable.
Therefore, it supports both parking and charging operations.

## Alternative Concept
If Vehicle were an interface, it could not provide a
constructor or maintain instance state in the same way
as an abstract class.

If Chargeable were an abstract class, Java's single class
inheritance would prevent ElectricCar from extending another
class at the same time.

## Complexity Analysis
- Parking fee calculation: O(1)
- Charging fee calculation: O(1)
- Space per vehicle: O(1) for a fixed number of fields.

## Conclusion
Abstraction hides implementation details and separates
common vehicle behaviour from specialised capabilities.